---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EchoRing from '../../components/EchoRing.astro';
import EchoWell from '../../components/EchoWell.astro';
import EchoTrain from '../../components/EchoTrain.astro';
import EchoNoise from '../../components/EchoNoise.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
// The paper this page is built on lives in the publications collection, so its links stay in one place.
const paper = (await getCollection('publications')).find((p) => p.data.id === 'gw-echoes')?.data;
const doi = paper?.doi ? `https://doi.org/${paper.doi}` : 'https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.98.024023';
const arxiv = paper?.url ?? 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.04877';
---
<Base title="Finding echoes" description="What a gravitational-wave echo would look like, where it would come from, and how to search for one without knowing its shape. Four things to play with, from my MSc thesis." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Finding echoes</h1>
    <p>
      In September 2015 the two LIGO detectors recorded, for the first time, a ripple in space itself:
      two black holes, each about thirty times the mass of the Sun, had spiralled into each other more
      than a billion years ago. The ripple stretched and squeezed the detectors' four-kilometre arms by
      less than a thousandth of the width of a proton. This page is about a question that came up soon
      after: how sure are we that what merged were black holes, and what would we hear if they were not?
    </p>
    <p>
      I worked on that question for my master's thesis at Nikhef in Amsterdam, and the method became
      a <a href={doi}>paper</a> with Ka Wa Tsang and colleagues. Below, the story in four animations.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stretch">What a gravitational wave does</h2>
    <p>
      Put a ring of free-floating masses in space and let a gravitational wave pass through, straight at
      you. The ring is stretched one way and squeezed the other, then the reverse, once per wave cycle.
      There are two ways to do this, called the plus and cross polarisations: the second is the first
      turned by 45 degrees. A real wave carries a mix of both. From a pair of black holes seen face-on
      the two arrive a quarter of a cycle apart, and the stretch direction spins round.
    </p>
    <EchoRing />
    <p>
      The four highlighted masses are where the ends of a detector's arms would sit. A LIGO detector is
      essentially two of those masses, one at the end of each arm, with a laser measuring the distance
      between them. The effect here is exaggerated by about twenty orders of magnitude; the real stretch
      was one part in 10<sup>21</sup>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ringdown">What a merger sounds like</h2>
    <p>
      The signal from two merging black holes has three parts. The <em>inspiral</em>: a rising whistle
      as the pair circles ever faster. The <em>merger</em>: the loudest moment, when they touch.
      And the <em>ringdown</em>: the newly formed black hole is lopsided at first and rings like a struck bell
      while it settles, for a few milliseconds. Then nothing. A black hole has a horizon, a surface of no
      return, so whatever falls in stays in and the bell goes quiet.
    </p>

    <h2 id="origin">What if it is not quite a black hole?</h2>
    <p>
      Nobody has seen a horizon. We have seen objects that are dark, heavy and compact enough to have
      one, and the ringdown of the 2015 merger looks exactly like general relativity says a black hole's
      should. But theorists have proposed several kinds of object that would look the same from far
      away and yet have no horizon: stars made of exotic particles, bubbles of vacuum energy held in by
      a shell of matter, wormholes. Collectively, exotic compact objects. What they share is a surface
      of some kind sitting a hair's breadth outside where the horizon would have been.
    </p>
    <p>
      That surface changes the ending of the story. The ringing happens at the <em>light ring</em>, an
      orbit at one and a half times the horizon radius where light can circle the hole, and it runs both
      ways: outward, as the ringdown we hear, and inward. A black hole swallows the inward part. An
      object with a surface bounces it back up to the light ring, which for waves acts as a barrier: a
      fraction of the energy leaks out, the rest is reflected back down, and so on. The result is a
      train of echoes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <EchoWell />
  <div class="measure">
    <p>
      The gap between echoes is the time a wave needs to go from the light ring down to the surface
      and back. The drawing measures distance the way the wave does, in travel time, and because time
      runs so slowly that deep in the gravity well, a surface closer to the horizon is further away:
      push the surface down and the wall moves off from the barrier. But the gap depends only on
      the <em>logarithm</em> of how close the surface sits to the would-be horizon: move the slider from a
      hundred-thousandth of the radius to a Planck length, thirty-five powers of ten, and the gap
      merely grows eightfold. For a remnant like the 2015 one, roughly sixty times the mass of the Sun,
      that is about a tenth of a second between echoes whatever the details. Long compared with the few
      milliseconds of ringdown, short enough to look for right after the main signal. The leak slider
      sets how much of the energy escapes at each bounce; the rest comes round again, quieter each time.
      Set the object to a black hole and the inward part falls through the horizon: one burst, then nothing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="shape">A shape without a theory</h2>
    <p>
      The trouble with searching for echoes is that nobody can tell you exactly what one looks like.
      Each candidate object predicts a different waveform, most of them only roughly, and the right
      object may be one nobody has written down. What the pictures do agree on are four fingerprints:
      the echoes arrive at a <strong>constant time gap</strong>, each is <strong>quieter</strong> than
      the last because the barrier only leaks a fraction, each is <strong>longer and lower in pitch</strong>
      because the barrier lets low frequencies through more easily, and each may pick up
      a <strong>phase shift</strong> from the bounce off the surface. The first two are on solid ground;
      the widening is softer, and the phase is a guess.
    </p>
    <p>
      So my thesis took a search that assumes no waveform at all and taught it those four fingerprints.
      BayesWave, an algorithm by Neil Cornish and Tyson Littenberg, describes whatever is in the data as
      a sum of small wave packets, sine-Gaussians, and lets Bayesian statistics decide how many are
      needed. We replaced the single packet by a <em>train</em> of packets, with the gap, damping,
      widening and phase step as knobs the algorithm turns while it fits. Here are those knobs.
    </p>
  </div>
  <EchoTrain />
  <div class="measure">
    <p>
      The dashed lines are the envelopes of the individual echoes; the small ticks mark their centres.
      Switch on the spectrum and the echoes show up as a comb: a signal that repeats every Δt has
      frequency teeth 1/Δt apart, which is the second way an echo search can recognise a train. The
      listen button plays the train pitched up three times and slowed down five times, because a
      170-hertz ping forty milliseconds apart is a low buzz rather than a series of echoes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="search">Pulling it out of the noise</h2>
    <p>
      In a real detector the echoes would be buried. How buried is measured by the signal-to-noise
      ratio, SNR: roughly, how many times louder than the noise a perfect filter would find the signal.
      The 2015 ringdown alone had an SNR of about 8. The thesis planted echo trains into simulated
      LIGO noise at SNRs of 8, 12, 18 and 25 and asked the algorithm to compare three explanations of
      the data: only noise, a glitch in one detector, or a real signal seen by both. The comparison
      comes out as a Bayes factor, the odds of the signal explanation against the noise explanation.
    </p>
  </div>
  <EchoNoise />
  <div class="measure">
    <p>
      The trace is the data in units of the noise level, so drag the SNR slider and watch the echoes
      surface out of the same noise. The train buried here is whatever you built in the figure above.
      "Show fit" runs a small cousin of BayesWave on the data: it builds whatever stands out of the
      noise from wave packets, one at a time, keeps a packet only when it stands well above anything
      noise alone produces, and knows nothing about echoes. At SNR 8 it usually finds nothing. As the
      SNR rises it picks up the first burst, then the echoes one by one, and reads off their spacing.
      The gauge underneath places the SNR on the log Bayes factors the thesis measured
      for these injections. At SNR 8 the runs were indistinguishable from pure noise; from SNR 12 the
      signal explanation won clearly; from around 15 the algorithm also measured the echoes: at SNR 25
      it read back a gap of 40 ± 7 ms, a damping of 0.69 ± 0.05 and a widening of 1.16 ± 0.09
      from a train planted with 40 ms, 0.7 and 1.2. The phase step it could not measure at all. One
      finding worth being upfront about: the trains did not <em>detect</em> echoes any better than
      plain BayesWave with single packets did. What they added is the reading of the fingerprints.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two honest caveats. The noise here is white, while real detector noise is coloured and full of
      glitches, which is exactly why the glitch hypothesis is in the comparison. And the gauge is an
      interpolation of four measured points for one family of injections, not a general law. Others
      have reported tentative hints of echoes in the real LIGO data, at a significance that did not
      survive closer scrutiny; no echo has been found. The point of a shape-free search is to be ready
      when a louder event comes, without betting on one theory of what a non-black-hole would be.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reading">Read more</h2>
    <ul class="pubs">
      <li>
        <a href={doi}>A morphology-independent data analysis method for detecting and characterizing gravitational wave echoes</a>
        <div class="meta">K. W. Tsang, <strong>M. Rollier</strong>, A. Ghosh, A. Samajdar, M. Agathos, K. Chatziioannou, V. Cardoso, G. Khanna, C. Van Den Broeck</div>
        <div class="meta">Physical Review D 98, 024023, 2018 · <a href={arxiv}>preprint</a></div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.3835">BayesWave: Bayesian inference for gravitational wave bursts and instrument glitches</a>
        <div class="meta">N. J. Cornish, T. B. Littenberg, 2015. The algorithm this work modifies.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.05363">Testing the nature of dark compact objects: a status report</a>
        <div class="meta">V. Cardoso, P. Pani, 2019. The review to read on exotic compact objects and echoes.</div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.00266">Echoes from the Abyss</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.09966">Low significance of evidence for black hole echoes</a>
        <div class="meta">J. Abedi, H. Dykaar, N. Afshordi, 2017; J. Westerweck et al., 2017. The tentative claim and its re-analysis.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="meta">
      MSc thesis, University of Amsterdam, VU Amsterdam and Nikhef, 2018, supervised by Chris Van Den
      Broeck, Archisman Ghosh and Ka Wa Tsang. Keys once a figure has focus: space plays and pauses the
      animations, r resets or rerolls the noise, s toggles the spectrum or the signal overlay, b the fit,
      l listens, f goes fullscreen. The address bar follows the settings, so a set-up can be linked.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
